virglrenderer: Venus command buffers hold the views their passes named

What: a command buffer holds the image views, their images, the framebuffers and render
passes that its render pass begins, dynamic renderings and a secondary's inheritance
named, until the driver let go of what it recorded: a reset, a new begin, a free, a pool
reset or destroy, the context's end. An image view holds its image. The app may destroy a
held object (the command buffer becomes invalid, which Vulkan allows): its id leaves
vkr's table at once and the driver's destroy waits for the last holder. Memory dedicated
to an image still goes first. A command buffer may hold objects of its own device only
(the begin infos' commonparent VUIDs); a view of another device's image is refused at
its create (VUID-vkCreateImageView-image-parent). vkDestroyCommandPool lets the driver free
the pool's command buffers before vkr lets go of what they held.
Why: KosmicKrisp reads a pass's views (and a view's image) where it splits a pass while
recording: barriers with more than one sample or a split, draws after a color map or
sample location change, the store ops at the pass's end. MoltenVK's recorded commands
keep raw image view, framebuffer and render pass pointers and read them when it encodes
at vkQueueSubmit. A view the app destroyed under a recorded command buffer was freed
memory there. The pass-views checks at the begin, a next subpass and the end stay.
Not covered (ADR 0065): views reached only through descriptor sets (the probe case
records what MoltenVK does), and what a pending KosmicKrisp command buffer reads on the
GPU (Metal 4 command buffers keep no texture alive).
Upstream: OmacVM policy (object lifetime VUIDs).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-commands.c (the c-state-destroyed-view-held- cases, also
under libgmalloc; c-probe-descriptor-view); --time: vkCmdBeginRenderPass + vkCmdEndRenderPass.

